hey David California's primary has been  moved up by three months from June to  March what impact will this have  specifically on the 2020 election  assuming that Donald Trump remains the  Republican nominee for 2020 okay so yes  March 3rd 2020 I believe is the new date  of the primary in California if Trump is  the Republican nominee the primary in  California really is going to matter  only insofar as who wins the Democratic  primary you still will have Iowa New  Hampshire Nevada and South Carolina  having their primaries before March 3rd  which is the date California will have  their primary along with a bunch of  other states Alabama Massachusetts North  Carolina Tennessee Oklahoma Texas  Vermont and Virginia so a few different  things to keep in mind number one  California does have a history of moving  around their primary even though  recently it has been in June in 2008 the  California primary was in February  they've had it at different times over  the last several election cycles the  impact could actually be significant for  a few different reasons number one if  you win big in California on March 3rd  that can have much more of a momentum  setting impact than it would have to  have California determine the result of  their primary in June so California  could play a much bigger role in  determining who the Democratic nominee  and ultimate challenger to Donald Trump  is going to be that's number one number  two remember that California allocates  delegates proportionally so if you get a  plurality of the California primary vote  with say it's a five-way primary with  34% you don't get all of Californians  Delic California's delegates you get 34  percent roughly of those delegates or as  closely as they can be sort of sliced up  so although there is importance to  California moving their primary up  because of that momentum setting agenda  on the other hand remember that unless  you overwhelmingly win California it's  not necessarily it's not likely to be  the deciding factor in who ultimately is  the Democratic nominee you've still got  to do really  well in the subsequent primaries in  particular in 2020 there are two primary  ways primary that the California primary  being in March could impact the  presidential campaign in general number  one if Kamala Harris runs senator from  California there is a case to be made  that if we assume she would do  particularly well in her home state that  having an early California primary is  good for her specifically that may not  ultimately be the case because  California is a major Democratic  fundraising stronghold stronghold could  be good for establishment Democrats with  more name recognition it could be good  for a more progressive candidate like  Bernie Sanders or Elizabeth Warren  although not all of California is that  progressive so I don't know but  potentially if Camilla Harris is a  candidate that could be an important  factor for California having an earlier  primary the bigger picture though is  that it's going to be really tough to  unseat Donald Trump I mean in general  it's just hard to defeat incumbent  presidents we've talked about this  before george w bush is a recent example  with everything going wrong in a failed  war in iraq still unable to be defeated  although we could say it came down to  foul play in ohio and a hundred and  forty thousand votes but that being said  it was close enough that that made a  difference so having a much longer  period of time having eight full months  if indeed we know who the Democratic  nominee is going to be by the middle of  March which we may we may not as a  result of this that gives a full eight  months to have one candidate running  against Donald Trump is that better  probably it's also a longer period of  time during which that candidate can  make errors during which that candidate  can be successfully smeared but that is  an important dynamic for unseating an  incumbent president particularly one  like Donald Trump who is appealing to  people not on the basis of what he's  actually doing but on the basis of  basically weaponizing disinformation  calling anybody who disagrees with you  fake news citing accomplishments that  aren't real  congratulating troops for a pay raise  this week that is imaginary so those are  a few of the thoughts I have about  California moving their primary up I do  want to hear from you what do you think  the effect will be of a three month  earlier California primary
 

